`#const` — Cross-Module Library Half
====================================

This is the **library half** of the cross-module compile-time
evaluation pin. It declares a handful of `#const fn` decls in a
dedicated module so the consumer file (`const_xmod_main.lsysl`)
can fold them inside its `const` bindings at compile time.

A non-generic `#const fn` body round-trips through `.smeta` so any
importing module can re-analyze it locally and feed it to the
const-evaluation driver. This file has no `#test` decls of its
own — it only provides material for the consumer to bind against.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features.effects.const_xmod_lib


### Scalar `#const fn`

The minimal cross-module case — a one-argument `#const fn`
returning an integer. The consumer binds `const X: int = cxm_double(21)`.

    #const
    cxm_double(x: int) -> int = x * 2


### Chained `#const fn`

A second function in the same library — used by the consumer to
pin that two cross-module calls can be folded in a single
binding. Also used internally to demonstrate that one `#const fn`
in the library can call another in the same library and still
re-analyze correctly downstream.

    #const
    cxm_inc(x: int) -> int = x + 1

    #const
    cxm_double_then_inc(x: int) -> int = cxm_inc(cxm_double(x))


### Aggregate-returning `#const fn`

An `#const fn` returning a fixed-size array. The consumer binds
`const T: [4]int = cxm_squares()`, materializing the table as
static data in the consumer's compilation unit.

    #const
    cxm_squares() -> [4]int
        var t: [4]int
        var i = 0
        while i < 4 do
            t[i] = i * i
            i = i + 1
        return t


### Float `#const fn`

A float-returning `#const fn` to pin the f64 round-trip across the
SMETA boundary.

    #const
    cxm_to_half(x: f64) -> f64 = x * 0.5
